\begin_layout Subsection
\noindent
Reading a Mega2 database into R
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\noindent
After you have the database, start up the R program.
 The first R commands you should type are:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\noindent
\begin_inset Flex Chunk
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

<<read1,eval=FALSE>>=
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

library(mega2r)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

ENV = read.Mega2DB("seqsimr.db", verbose = TRUE)
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\noindent
The first argument should be the path to the database.
 Providing the optional argument, verbose, causes the read function to summarize
 the tables created, their fields and their sizes.
 Finally, an "R environment" that contains the database tables is returned.
 (If you are unfamiliar with environments, you can think of them as data
 frames.
 If fact, ENV$locus_table will access the locus_table variable from ENV
 as though fetching an "observation" from a data frame.
 The difference is when you change a data frame passed to a function, the
 change does not affect the original data frame.
 Only the function's local value is changed; ALL changes are forgotten when
 the function exits.
 If you change the data in an environment passed to a function, the change
 is permanent.) 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\noindent
\begin_inset Flex Chunk
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

<<read2>>=
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

library(mega2r)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

ENV = read.Mega2DB("seqsimr.db", verbose = TRUE)
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\noindent
We need to make two observations that apply to all Mega2R functions: The
 first is that when verbose is set in the initial read.Mega2DB, the value
 will be remembered.
 It may be used by any subsequent function.
 If verbose is TRUE, Mega2R functions will possibly print out far more diagnosti
c information than you might need, especially on subsequent runs.
 The second is that all Mega2R functions that do not return an environment,
 need to have an environment supplied as an argument.
 There are two ways to accomplish this.
 If you read "
\emph on
seqsimr.db
\emph default
" into the variable 
\emph on
seqsim
\emph default
r, then you could supply as the named argument 
\series bold
envir
\series default
 the value 
\emph on
seqsimr
\emph default
 in the Mega2R function as in:
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
\noindent
showMega2ENV(envir = seqsimr)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\noindent
The second choice is a bit of a "hack" but it is very convenient.
 Every Mega2R function (that does not return an environment) has a named
 argument defined as
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
\noindent
envir = ENV
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\noindent
as in
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
\noindent
showMega2ENV = function(envir = ENV) { ...
 }
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\noindent
The code above, sets global variable 
\series bold
ENV
\series default
 to the local envir variable.
 And the argument evaluation mechanism of R will supports this useage; even
 though 
\series bold
ENV
\series default
 is not known until runtime.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
\noindent
Back to more examples.
\end_layout

