\begin_layout Subsection
\noindent
Running pedgene using the mega2pedgene R package
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\noindent
mega2pedgene is the exception that proves the rule.
 Rather than use the read.Mega2DB function described above and as used by
 all the other packages, we use init_pedgene.
 This function calls a utility function created for read.Mega2DB.
 Then it creates, edits, and rewrites the 
\series bold
fam
\series default
 data frame to purge persons with unknown case/control status.
 (
\series bold
fam
\series default
 merges data from the pedigree_table, person_table and phenotype_table as
 you might expect.) Finally, it calculates and stores some values that will
 be used repeatedly.
 viz:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\noindent
\begin_inset Flex Chunk
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

<<init_pedgene>>=
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

library(mega2pedgene)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

ENV = init_pedgene("seqsimr.db", verbose = T)
\end_layout

\end_inset

Mega2R has an internal default list of the chromosome and base pair ranges
 for a number of gene transcripts.
 These transcripts come from the UCSC Genome Browser reference assembly
 GRCH37.
 The list was further modified to eliminate multiple records of the same
 gene with the exact same transcript start and transcript end.
 This constitutes about 30,000 records.
 The function run_pedgene examines the first 100 transcripts and prints
 the results.
 However, our test data is from part of the first chromosome only; the first
 100 transcripts find no markers to run pedgene on.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\noindent
\begin_inset Flex Chunk
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

<<run_pedgene,eval=FALSE>>=
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

run_pedgene()
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\noindent
When we perform more thorough runs, below, you will see many reports of
 "No markers in range", but occasionally you will see a listing of a gene
 name, markers, and count of 1/1, 1/2 and 2/2 genotypes, viz.
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
\noindent
CEP104 snp24027 1280 97 3 
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
\noindent
CEP104 snp24788 1375 5 0 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\noindent
The genotypes for these markers, along with the markers info are fed to
 the call back function DOpedgene.
 DOpedgene converts the genotype nucleotides to the count of the minor allele
 and then runs pedgene for several different marker weights.
 The results are automatically stored in a file, "k_Schaid_rare.txt".
 Lines in that file have a prefix of chromosome, gene, number of markers
 and base pair range followed by 6 p-values.
 Two for each of three weightings of the markers.
 It is printed as well.
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
\noindent
chr1 CEP104 2 3728644 3773797 0.8939136 0.8047689 0.8155284 0.6827252 0.9473635
 0.9365409 1 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\noindent
It would be better to run pedgene on all the default transcript entries.
 So type:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\noindent
\begin_inset Flex Chunk
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

<<ranges1,eval=FALSE>>=
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

# we will skip this line for the Rmd document production because it takes
 too long
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

applyFnToRanges(DOpedgene, ENV$refRanges, ENV$refIndices, envir = ENV)
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\noindent
If you run the above test, you will see that gene ROR1 has some p-values
 less than 0.01, FAM129A less than 0.02, and NEK7 and IGSF21 less than 0.03.
 If we knew this fact to start with, and hence these were the only genes
 we were interested in.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\noindent
NOTE: You will observe that for several genes there are a few transcripts
 with different start and end values.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\noindent
A shorter test would be
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\noindent
\begin_inset Flex Chunk
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

<<ranges2>>=
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

# turn off verbose
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

ENV$verbose = FALSE
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

applyFnToRanges(DOpedgene, ENV$refRanges[1:300,], ENV$refIndices, envir
 = ENV)
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\noindent
Alternatively, you may try searching by Gene.
 Here the default transcript database is TxDb.Hsapiens.UCSC.hg19.knownGene"
 from Bioconductor.
 Of course you can change it.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\noindent
\begin_inset Flex Chunk
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

<<genes1>>=
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

applyFnToGenes(DOpedgene, genes_arg = c('ROR1', 'FAM129A', 'NEK7', 'IGSF21'),
 envir = ENV)
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\noindent
or
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\noindent
\begin_inset Flex Chunk
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

<<genes2>>=
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

applyFnToGenes(DOpedgene, genes_arg = c('GDAP2'), envir = ENV)
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
\noindent
Writing a VCF File using the mega2vcf R package
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\noindent
First create the directory "vcfr"; so we can keep all the generated data
 in one place and then load the library.
 Mega2VCF takes an argument which is the prefix used on all the files it
 generates.
 We have chosen to make that a path that includes a directory.
 Mega2VCF is explained in greater depth in the Implementation Section.
 The Mega2VCF function assumes the environment is contained in the global
 variable ENV.
 If this is not true supply an additional argument "envir" set to the environmen
t you wish to use.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\noindent
Note, that Mega2VCF will create the expected .vcf file as well as a .fam file
 (we do linkage), a .phe file (phenotypes) and a few others.
 These files are listed below.
 Creating the VCF file and related files is accomplished by typing:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\noindent
\begin_inset Flex Chunk
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

<<vcf>>=
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

if (! dir.exists("vcfr")) dir.create("vcfr")
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

library(mega2vcf)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

Mega2VCF("vcfr/vcf.01", ENV$markers[ENV$markers$chromosome==1 ,])
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

list.files("vcfr")
\end_layout

\end_inset

Read the package code to see how these files are created from the data frames.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
\noindent
Creating a GenABEL gwaa.data-class object using the mega2genabel R package
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\noindent
GenABEL can process PLINK .tped/.tfam files with the finction 
\emph on
convert.snp.tped
\emph default
 to create a gwaa.data-class object.
 Currently, we use this mechanism to convert our data frames to a gwaa.data-class
 object.
 The argument to Mega2GenABEL is a file name prefix that will be used to
 name the .tped/.fam files that are created as an intermediate to be read
 by GenABEL http://www.genabel.org/ 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\noindent
https://cran.r-project.org/web/packages/GenABEL/index.html to produce a GenABEL
 "raw" file.
 Then the "raw" file and a generated phenotype file are processed by 
\emph on
load.gwaa.data
\emph default
 to yield a gwaa.data-class object.
 (See the Implementation Section.) This task is analogous to the previous
 exercise.
 Type:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\noindent
\begin_inset Flex Chunk
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

<<genabel>>=
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

library(mega2genabel)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

seqsimgwaa = Mega2GenABEL("TMP")
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

list.files(pattern = "TMP.*")
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

str(seqsimgwaa)
\end_layout

\end_inset

You can use any of the GenABEL provided functions on your data, seqsimgwaa.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\noindent
Note: There are several data formats that Mega2 can read and transform into
 a database that are not directly acceptable to GenABEL.
 Any M1ega2 database that can be read into R, can be transformed to GenABEL
 following this example with seqsimr.db.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
\noindent
Regression
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\noindent
To verify that the R packages described above have worked correctly, we
 can compare their output files to those created by Mega2 itself, to verify
 that they are, as expected, identical.
 This type of testing is known as regression testing.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
\noindent
VCF regression
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\noindent
It is time to use the MEGA2.BATCH.vcf file from the mega2rtutorial.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\noindent
This will use the C++ Mega2 program to populate the vcf directory with the
 same set of files that the R program created in the vcfr directory.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\noindent
At the  prompt, type:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\noindent
\begin_inset Flex Chunk
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

<<mega2vcf,eval=FALSE>>=
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

system("mkdir vcf")
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

system("mega2 MEGA2.BATCH.vcf", intern=TRUE)
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

